1. What is Kubernetes:
    - Kubernetes (often abbreviated as K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform used to automate:
        - Deployment of applications
        - Scaling applications
        - Load balancing
        - Service discovery
        - Self-healing
        - Rolling updates and rollbacks
    - It was originally developed by Google and is now maintained by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation.

2. Why Kubernetes Exists:
    - Before Kubernetes, managing containers manually was difficult because:
        - Containers could crash unexpectedly
        - Scaling required manual intervention
        - Networking between containers was complex
        - Updates caused downtime
        - Infrastructure management became messy at scale
    - Kubernetes solves these problems by providing a centralized system to manage containerized applications.

3. Core Kubernetes Concepts
    - Cluster: A cluster is a group of machines working together.
        It contains:
            - Control Plane (Master Node) → manages the cluster
            - Worker Nodes → run applications
    - Node: A node is a machine (VM or physical server) inside the cluster.
        Two types:
            - Control Plane Node
            - Worker Node
    - Pod: A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
        - A pod usually contains:
            One container
            Shared networking
            Shared storage
        - Example:
            kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
        - This creates a pod running the NGINX container.
    - Deployment: A Deployment manages pods and ensures:
        - Desired number of replicas
        - Auto-recovery
        - Rolling updates
        - Example:
            apiVersion: apps/v1
            kind: Deployment
            metadata:
                name: nginx-deployment
            spec:
                replicas: 3
                selector:
                    matchLabels:
                        app: nginx
                template:
                    metadata:
                        labels:
                            app: nginx
                    spec:
                        containers:
                        - name: nginx
                            image: nginx
    - Service: A Service exposes pods to:
        - Internal cluster traffic
        - External users
        - Types:
            ClusterIP
            NodePort
            LoadBalancer

4. Kubernetes Architecture: High-Level Architecture
                        +-------------------+
                        |   Control Plane   |
                        |-------------------|
                        | API Server        |
                        | Scheduler         |
                        | Controller Manager|
                        | etcd              |
                        +---------+---------+
                                |
            -----------------------------------------
            |                                       |
    +--------+--------+                    +---------+--------+
    |   Worker Node   |                    |   Worker Node    |
    |-----------------|                    |------------------|
    | kubelet         |                    | kubelet          |
    | kube-proxy      |                    | kube-proxy       |
    | Container Runtime|                   | Container Runtime|
    | Pods            |                    | Pods             |
    +-----------------+                    +------------------+

    - Control Plane Components:
        - API Server: The API Server is the brain of Kubernetes.
            - Receives kubectl commands
            - Validates requests
            - Communicates with cluster components
            - Example: kubectl get pods
        - etcd: etcd is a distributed key-value database.
            - It stores:
                Cluster state
                Configuration
                Secrets
                Metadata
            - If etcd is lost, cluster state is lost.
        - Scheduler: The Scheduler decides:
            - “Which node should run this pod?”
            - It checks:
                CPU
                Memory
                Constraints
                Taints/tolerations
        - Controller Manager: Controllers continuously monitor cluster state.
            - Examples:
                Node Controller
                Deployment Controller
                ReplicaSet Controller
            - They ensure:
                Actual state = Desired state
    - Worker Node Components:
        - kubelet: The kubelet:
            Runs on every node
            Communicates with API server
            Ensures containers are running
        - kube-proxy
            Handles:
                Networking
                Service routing
                Load balancing
        - Container Runtime: Software that runs containers.
            Examples:
                containerd
                CRI-O
                (Docker support now works through CRI compatibility.)

5. Kubernetes Workflow Example:
    - Suppose you deploy an app with 3 replicas.
    - Step-by-step
        - User runs: kubectl apply -f app.yaml
        - API Server receives request
        - Deployment object stored in etcd
        - Scheduler selects worker nodes
        - kubelet starts containers
        - Pods become available
        - Service exposes application
        - If a pod crashes: Controller recreates it automatically
    - This is called self-healing.

6. Setting Up a Local Kubernetes Cluster
    - Install Requirements
        Linux Install:
            Docker
            kubectl
            Minikube
    - Install kubectl
        Linux example:
            curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s \
            https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
            chmod +x kubectl
            sudo mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/
        Verify:
            kubectl version --client
    - Install Minikube
        Linux example:
            curl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/minikube-linux-amd64
            sudo install minikube-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/minikube
        Verify:
            minikube version
    - Start Cluster
        minikube start
        - This:
            Creates VM/container
            Starts Kubernetes components
            Configures kubectl automatically
        - Check nodes:
            kubectl get nodes
            - Expected:
                NAME       STATUS   ROLES           AGE   VERSION
                minikube   Ready    control-plane   2m    v1.xx.x
